\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 A Free-Speech Lesson for \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 ; A federal judge scolds Illinois Democrats for trying to silence  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  foes.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 482 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 August 2023\par 02:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats are eager to make their states havens for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and in Illinois they're willing to violate free-speech rights along the way. That's the finding of a federal judge who has enjoined a law to silence groups that run pregnancy help centers.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  recently signed a law amending the state's Consumer Fraud and Deceptive Business Practices Act to sweep in pregnancy centers that counsel women against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The law, drafted by Illinois Attorney General \uc2 Kwame Raoul\uc2 , regulates the antiabortion groups on grounds they engage in "deceptive business practices."\par \par \uc2 The law "is both stupid and very likely unconstitutional," federal Judge Iain Johnston wrote in an Aug. 4 order granting a preliminary injunction. "It is stupid because its own supporter admitted it was unneeded" and had no evidence supporting its claims of deception, he wrote. "It is likely unconstitutional because it is a blatant example of government taking the side of whose speech is sanctionable and whose speech is immunized."\par \par \uc2 The speech of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers is "specifically excluded from being sanctioned under the Consumer Fraud Act," Judge Johnston wrote. Regulating pregnancy centers for deceptive practices while exempting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers is viewpoint discrimination. In Reed v. Town of Gilbert (2015), the Supreme Court held that laws regulating speech based on content are "presumptively unconstitutional" and violate the First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 The law was challenged by the National Institute of Family and Life Advocates and others. \uc2 John Jansen\uc2 , a project coordinator for Pro-Life Action League, submitted a Freedom of Information Act request to the AG's office seeking information about what fraudulent or misleading conduct by pregnancy centers had prompted the AG's crackdown.\par \par \uc2 His declaration to the court says the FOIA found no patient had "ever lodg(ed) an actual complaint with the Attorney General against an Illinois pregnancy help ministry, much less for a violation of the Consumer Fraud and Deceptive Business Practice Act."\par \par \uc2 Messrs. Raoul and Pritzker know that the sidewalk counselors and pregnancy centers aren't engaged in commercial transactions. Sidewalk counselors are typically volunteers and often from religious organizations. "There is no remuneration of any kind and there is no economic motivation of any kind," the judge noted in his order.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pritzker is gaining a reputation as a hard-left culture warrior who is happy to silence political opponents. He told CNN that the law is "just like the case against President Trump. You have a right to free speech, but you don't have a right to lie."\par \par \uc2 But special counsel Jack Smith's indictment specifically says Mr. Trump has a right to lie. Mr. Pritzker apparently thinks that invoking the name Trump is a justification to get away with saying or doing anything. Not under the U.S. Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230813ej8d002e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
