\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Ohio Vote Is Another Warning to GOP on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 ; Defeat of Issue 1 on Tuesday is likely to encourage  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates to put the issue on more ballots\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto and Aaron Zitner \par \uc2 1,289 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2023\par 21:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Even in red-leaning states, support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is again proving to be a potent electoral force.\par \par \uc2 That was the message of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ohio-election-abortion-issue-one-vote-d7df6402"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tuesday's vote in Ohio }}}\uc2 , where voters rejected a ballot measure that would have made it harder to add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to the state's constitution.\par \par \uc2 The victory for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups, which follows similar wins in Kansas and Michigan, is likely to encourage \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates to put the issue on ballots in more states next year and beyond. Such moves could boost Democratic turnout at a time when many party leaders are worried about low enthusiasm for re-electing President Biden because of his age and weak job-approval ratings.\par \par \uc2 Republicans likely will have to continue grappling with how much to respond to their conservative base on the issue given that efforts to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appear to alienate many moderate and independent voters, particularly in the suburbs.\par \par \uc2 The Ohio vote on the proposal known as Issue 1 didn't directly touch on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it would have put a significant hurdle in front of a separate measure\u8212\'20\'14on the November ballot\u8212\'20\'14to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state's constitution. Issue 1 would have raised the threshold to amend the state's constitution by referendum to 60% support, instead of a simple majority. As of Tuesday afternoon, with 97% of the ballots in, the vote stood at 57% against Issue 1 and 43% in support, according to the Associated Press.\par \par \uc2 Both antiabortion and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups ran millions of dollars of advertising in the state in recent weeks. Republican Ohio Secretary of State \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2 , who supported Issue 1, said it was needed to raise the standards for future amendments, but at a Lincoln Day event in May he said it was "100% about keeping a radical, pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment out of our constitution."\par \par \uc2 Kimberly Inez McGuire\uc2 , executive director of URGE, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights group, said Issue 1 was "a cynical attempt to sabotage the power of the people to secure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and they failed."\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What does the vote in Ohio mean for the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on a national level? Join the conversation below.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-dobbs-year-after-roe-support-politics-d1ef5a5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have prevailed in six ballot measures }}}\uc2  since the overturning of Roe v. Wade last June. But a key question has been whether they can continue winning referendums in conservative and politically mixed states, as well as leverage voter passions about the issue in other political races.\par \par \uc2 "It's pretty clear what direction this stuff is going in. I do think it sends a pretty strong incentive for Democrats to get that on the ballot," especially in the 2024 presidential election, said Susan Burgess, distinguished professor emerita of political science at \uc2 Ohio University\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters are gathering signatures in Florida to put a measure on the ballot enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in that state's constitution in 2024, which would go before voters as GOP Sen. \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  is seeking re-election. A similar effort is under way in Arizona, one of the most competitive presidential swing states and home to one of the year's marquee Senate races. That measure could also appear on the November 2024 ballot.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have struggled with how to frame the issue. GOP presidential hopefuls are under pressure from antiabortion groups, an important source of political support, to support a national 15-week ban. So far, leading candidates, including Florida Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , have avoided committing to a national ban, although some of their rivals have.\par \par \uc2 In a warning sign for Republicans nationally, the Ohio results signaled that support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights can drive voter turnout. The defeat of Issue 1, by a landslide 14 percentage points, came as more than 3 million voters cast ballots. It was a large turnout for a summertime, off-year election, and substantially higher than the state's most recent primary election, in May 2022.\par \par \uc2 "It is a motivating factor for voters who tend to be more liberal, and what you don't want is something on the ballot that will turn them out,'' said John Truscott, who worked for former Republican Gov. John Engler of Michigan and now runs a communications firm. "It's a losing proposition for Republicans.''\par \par \uc2 The results also suggested that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions draw wide opposition not only in the nation's populous cities and suburbs but also the exurbs\u8212\'20\'14the communities on the edges of the suburbs that Republicans have relied on to produce big voting margins. Lately, those margins have slipped. The nation's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/joe-biden-gained-trump-stalled-in-the-nations-suburbs-11604937011"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 exurbs swung away from Trump }}}\uc2 between 2016 and 2020, trimming his 18 point margin from 2016 by 6 percentage points, a Wall Street Journal analysis found after the last presidential election.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, outside Columbus, Trump won exurban Delaware County\u8212\'20\'14one of the state's most populous\u8212\'20\'14by 7 points in 2020; voters there rejected Issue 1 by 16 points. Voters were split about evenly on the measure in Fairfield County, an exurb of Columbus, and Butler County, an exurb of Cincinnati. Trump had won both in 2020 by more than 20 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 The results also showed that the nation's urban-rural differences remain an important dividing line in politics. In the 10 Ohio counties that each produced more than 80,000 votes, Issue 1 failed in nine. In the 50 counties that each produced fewer than 15,000 votes, voters favored the measure in all but two.\par \par \uc2 It remains far from clear whether Tuesday's results will lead to a revival of Democratic fortunes in Ohio or other similarly conservative states.\par \par \uc2 "Right now you're seeing a lot of Democrats take victory laps in the state" and suggesting Ohio may now be more of a swing state, said Robert Alexander, founding director of the Institute for Civics and Public Policy at \uc2 Ohio Northern University\uc2 . "That might be a bridge too far."\par \par \uc2 Democrats can look to Democratic Michigan Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , who won re-election by more than 10 points in a race that turned in large part on her opponent's support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and featured an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 But several GOP governors handily won re-election last year after backing strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, including Govs. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  of Georgia and Greg Abbott of Texas. In Florida, DeSantis won re-election by nearly 20 percentage points after signing a ban on most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. He later signed a six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's vote in Ohio was also likely affected by factors other than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Many voters across the political spectrum viewed it as an attack on democracy and the citizens' veto, political scientists said.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, polling in the state shows strong support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Opponents of the procedure said that in the lead-up to the November vote they plan to focus on arguments that the amendment would abrogate parental rights.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, \uc2 Frank Pavone\uc2 , national director of Priests for Life, acknowledged it will be an uphill battle for the antiabortion side, which is likely to be outspent and doesn't have the wind at its back in these types of races nationally.\par \par \uc2 "Every state battle has to be now and is de facto a national battle," he said. "It creates a certain momentum, a certain expectation, a certain emboldening of one side or another."\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Aaron Zitner at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:aaron.zitner@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aaron.zitner@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230809ej890038p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
