\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Ohio Is Another GOP  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Warning; Democrats keep winning referendums, by a lot, even in Republican-leaning states.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 587 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2023\par 16:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The ballot question in Ohio on Tuesday said nothing about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it was a proxy battle, and everyone knew it. The side arguing for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights won by 14 points in a state President Trump carried three years ago by eight points. Ohio joins Kansas, Michigan and Wisconsin as warnings to Republicans about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics.\par \par \uc2 The measure Ohioans rejected, called Issue 1, would have raised the threshold for amending the state's constitution to 60% of voters. It's hardly a crazy idea in principle. The founders were wary of direct democracy, and sometimes it runs off the policy rails. Yet Issue 1 was rejected, 57% to 43%.\par \par \uc2 The context is what's coming on the ballot this November: an amendment to put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the Ohio constitution. That now can pass with a simple majority. "Every individual has a right to make and carry out one's own reproductive decisions," the amendment says. If approved, the state couldn't unduly "burden" this right. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be prohibited after fetal viability," except if a physician believes it's necessary "to protect the pregnant patient's life or health."\par \par \uc2 That would be an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right more extreme than what prevailed under Roe v. Wade. Ohio has a law generally requiring parental consent for a minor seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Does this "burden" the \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  of an "individual"? Would the "health" exception for late-term abortions be elastic enough to include an assessment of the mother's mental, financial or familial health? Such complications are an example of why the people delegate lawmaking to elected representatives.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps debate on these questions will give Ohioans pause, but they echo criticisms that bounced off Michigan's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment, which passed 57% to 43% last year. That was months after Kansans decided to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state constitution, 59% to 41%. A similar dynamic in Wisconsin helped Democrats flip a state Supreme Court seat in April, 55% to 44%.\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion organizations are fooling themselves if they think this won't continue, or indulge the excuse that they lost in Ohio merely because they were outspent. The latter won't change. On Tuesday a group called Arizona for \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access filed paperwork for an amendment there. Democrats see this as a route to the policy they want, but they also intend to use \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendums to juice turnout in 2024 and help candidates up and down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Republicans spent half a century working to overturn Roe, yet they weren't prepared for the democratic policy debate when that finally happened in Dobbs last year. Now they're seeing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes as loose as Roe, or potentially looser, imposed by voters even in conservative states. This political liability will persist until the GOP finds an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  message that most voters can accept.\par \par \uc2 One happy camper Wednesday was Ohio Democratic Sen. \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , who's facing a tough re-election campaign in 2024. He has new evidence that he can capitalize on turnout driven by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But note Mr. Brown's revealing statement Tuesday night that Ohioans had kept democracy "in the hands of voters."\par \par \uc2 That's exactly what Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 's opinion in Dobbs did, empowering voters, when Democrats wanted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to be set by unelected judges. Mr. Brown won't admit it, but Justice Alito's constitutional honesty could help save his Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230809ej89002e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
