\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Senate Passes Defense Bill, Setting Up Fight Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Transgender Care; Democratic-led $886 billion legislation excludes social-policy measures favored by House Republicans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lindsay Wise and Simon J. Levien \par \uc2 1,254 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 July 2023\par 01:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The Democratic-led Senate passed its version of the annual defense-policy bill with broad bipartisan support, putting the legislation on a collision course with the Republican-controlled House, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/mccarthy-rolls-dice-on-defense-vote-as-culture-wars-split-house-1b8a638"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 narrowly voted earlier this month  }}}\uc2  to add contentious provisions that would restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and transgender healthcare for troops.\par \par \uc2 The vote was 86-11.\par \par \uc2 "What's happening in the Senate is a stark contrast to the partisan race to the bottom we saw in the House, where House Republicans are pushing partisan legislation that has zero chance of passing," said Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  (D., N.Y.), ahead of the Senate vote. Schumer noted that the Senate process included votes on 98 amendments, many of them bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 "This is really important for our country," said Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  (R., Ky.).\par \par \uc2 The Senate's National Defense Authorization Act for fiscal 2024, or NDAA, does share some central similarities with the House-passed version: Both would authorize $886 billion in spending on national security, including a 5.2% pay raise for service members and the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  's civilian workforce, and green light $300 million in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ukraines-stalled-offensive-puts-biden-in-uneasy-political-position-6f686ab5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 security assistance for Ukraine  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But senators largely sidestepped {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/house-gop-threatens-to-derail-defense-bill-over-abortion-transgender-care-1c7dfe7a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the polarizing social issues  }}}\uc2  that had roiled the House a few weeks earlier, when members of the ultraconservative Freedom Caucus demanded and won amendment votes related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and transgender care for service members.\par \par \uc2 Both amendments narrowly passed the House, mostly along party lines. One would overturn a Pentagon policy allowing troops leave and travel funds for reproductive healthcare\u8212\'20\'14including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14and the other would prevent the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  or Tricare, the military's healthcare program, from providing gender-related surgeries and hormone treatments for transgender people.\par \par \uc2 House Republicans said they were delivering on their promise to end what they saw as the Biden administration's inappropriate "social experiment" on the military, while Democrats complained that Republicans had hijacked a national-security bill to push a far-right political agenda.\par \par \uc2 Senators mostly avoided the controversy\u8212\'20\'14for now\u8212\'20\'14by skipping floor votes on any amendments dealing with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and transgender treatments in the military.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  (R., La.), who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said adding an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment was too controversial to consider. "One way to gum up the works at this point is to get off into divisive issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Kennedy said.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What do you think of the provisions included in the defense-policy bill? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers will now work behind closed doors to negotiate a compromise that combines the House and Senate NDAAs. Typically centrists from both parties come together to ensure the final product of these talks can pass both chambers by large margins, but the process could be complicated this year by divided government and culture-war politics as the country heads into next year's elections.\par \par \uc2 The NDAA increases funding for military recruiting and advertising, implements higher standards for enlisted barracks and boosts investments in microelectronics, hypersonic weapons and drones.\par \par \uc2 One amendment, added to the bill with support from senators of both parties, would prevent any president from leaving the \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\uc2  without Senate approval.\par \par \uc2 Other bipartisan provisions would give the administration emergency powers to stop the trafficking of fentanyl and strengthen the Treasury Department's enforcement over the use of crypto in illicit finance.\par \par \uc2 Additional notable measures relate to:\par \par \uc2 Aid to Taiwan\par \par \uc2 As with last year's authorization, the Senate NDAA would bolster U.S. assistance to Taiwan. The Senate version envisions a closer relationship between the U.S. and Taiwan militaries, with the bill requiring the defense secretary to develop a comprehensive training program for Taiwan's armed forces. That effort, the bill says, should enhance the two militaries' capabilities to operate jointly and encourage information sharing.\par \par \uc2 The bill also aims to push forward a measure in last year's defense authorization that gave the president the authority to draw from U.S. military stockpiles to aid Taiwan. Implementation has been slow, in part because the administration is already drawing on those stocks to arm Ukraine. To address that, this year's bill authorizes replenishment of those stockpiles once tapped, whether for Ukraine or Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investment in farmland\par \par \uc2 The Senate added a provision largely preventing foreign entities from China, Russia, Iran or North Korea from buying U.S. farmland, in response to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/farmland-becomes-flashpoint-in-u-s-china-relations-11675652368"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 national security concerns about such purchases  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The measure also authorizes the \uc2 Committee on Foreign Investment in the U.S .\uc2  , or \uc2 Cfius\uc2  , to review foreigners' purchases or leases of more than 320 acres of farmland, or those valued at $5 million or more. The multiagency panel {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-orders-deeper-scrutiny-of-foreign-investment-in-tech-and-supply-chains-11663246802"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 can advise the president to block or unwind foreign acquisitions  }}}\uc2  for security concerns. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich427\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6405
