\par \par \uc2 A Chinese company had planned to build a corn mill on a plot of land in Grand Forks, N.D., before the project was deemed a U.S. national-security risk. PHOTO: Lewis Ableidinger for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 U.S. investment in China\par \par \uc2 One provision would require American investors to notify the Treasury Department about stakes they take in certain Chinese technology sectors, including artificial intelligence, advanced semiconductors and satellite communications. The Biden administration has also been working on an executive order that would in some ways go further than the Senate amendment by banning certain investments in China\u8212\'20\'14as well as requiring disclosure of others. The goal of the efforts is to stop American money and knowledge from helping China develop technology that could be used for military purposes.\par \par \uc2 UFO documents\par \par \uc2 Another bipartisan amendment that made it into the legislation would give federal agencies 300 days to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/rumors-flying-around-about-ufos-prompt-push-for-release-of-files-6477de49"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hand over documents  }}}\uc2  related to unidentified anomalous phenomena to a newly established review board with the power to declassify them. The proposal is modeled after a 1992 law declassifying documents related to \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2  's assassination, requiring public release within 25 years.\par \par \uc2 AI push\par \par \uc2 Several provisions relate to artificial intelligence, which Schumer described as "early initial steps" in his push for the Senate to legislate on AI, in response to mass consumer adoption of ChatGPT and other new AI systems. It calls for two federal studies of AI risks, one covering vulnerabilities in AI-enabled military applications and the other looking at use of AI in the financial industry. It also tells the Pentagon to create a "bug bounty" program offering rewards for identifying flaws in large AI systems being integrated into military operations.\par \par \uc2 Microchip\uc2  manufacturing\par \par \uc2 A proposal led by Sens. Mark Kelly (D., Ariz.) and \uc2 Todd Young\uc2  (R., Ind.) would speed up permitting projects for domestic microchip manufacturing by streamlining approval for projects already under construction, such as two manufacturing centers in Arizona for chip manufacturers Intel and \uc2 Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Healthcare for 9/11 first responders and survivors\par \par \uc2 Senators voted to deliver nearly $700 million to close a funding gap for the World Trade Center Health Program that provides medical treatment and monitoring for 9/11 first responders and survivors. The provision also would for the first time expand eligibility to \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  employees who served at the Pentagon and the United 93 crash site on Sept. 11, 2001.\par \par \uc2 Charles Hutzler, Ryan Tracy, Andrew Duehren and Kristina Peterson contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lindsay Wise at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:lindsay.wise@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lindsay.wise@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Simon J. Levien at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:simon.levien@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 simon.levien@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Simon J. Levien at \par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230728ej7s00001\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
