\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Texas Women Denied Abortions Testify About Impact of State Bans; One woman recounted suffering septic shock, while another said she gave birth to a baby that had no chance to live\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto and Adolfo Flores \par \uc2 860 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 July 2023\par 22:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AUSTIN, Texas\u8212\'20\'14For the first time since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2 , women provided hours of testimony in front of a judge about the emotional and physical impact of being denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of a state ban.\par \par \uc2 Three women, part of a group challenging \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in Texas, took the witness stand in Austin and recounted stories of suffering harrowing complications during wanted pregnancies and being told by physicians their options were limited by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 state's criminal and civil \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans }}}\uc2 . The women said they were forced to wait until their conditions deteriorated, travel out of state, or carry nonviable fetuses to term.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Zurawski testified that she suffered a premature rupture of membranes that meant her unborn daughter wouldn't survive but doctors told her they wouldn't be able to terminate the pregnancy unless the heartbeat stopped or Zurawski showed signs of acute infection.\par \par \uc2 "Several times I just had to listen to her heartbeat, while simultaneously wanting to hear it and not wanting to hear it at the same time," Zurawski said. She said she ultimately went into septic shock and spent three days in intensive care.\par \par \uc2 Battles are being fought in state courts around the U.S. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-dobbs-year-after-roe-support-politics-d1ef5a5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 over the future of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access }}}\uc2 , but in most cases court hearings have focused on arguments about the textual interpretation and historical context of state constitutions, not extensive real-world testimony.\par \par \uc2 Most of the 15 plaintiffs in the Texas case are women who have been denied the procedure, allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates to put the women's experiences on display. Wednesday's witnesses frequently broke down in tears, and one vomited while testifying.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs argue that inconsistent and nonmedical language in Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statutes has created confusion for physicians about when abortions are permitted in medical emergencies, putting women's life and liberty at risk in violation of the state's constitution. They are asking state District Judge Jessica Mangrum to immediately block enforcement of the law in such situations and are ultimately looking to the courts to create a standard under which doctors can use their medical judgment to determine whether a patient's life or health is at risk if an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't performed.\par \par \uc2 The case represents a targeted legal strategy for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates, one that might help them carve out incremental victories in conservative states where courts are unlikely to strike down \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans entirely. Even if the Texas lawsuit is successful, women generally won't be able to seek abortions for unwanted pregnancies and the procedure would become more accessible only in cases where pregnant patients suffer rare complications.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters have some record of success on this issue even in conservative states, such as North Dakota and Oklahoma, which have recognized a narrow right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state constitutions in medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Texas has responded to the suit saying that any delays in medical care the women experienced were the result of decisions by individual doctors, not state officials. No provider has been prosecuted for providing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a medical emergency in Texas, state attorneys said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 They said the lawsuit is essentially a public-relations campaign for supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. "The plaintiffs simply do not like Texas' restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," state attorney Amy Pletscher told the judge.\par \par \uc2 Two women testified about experiences of having fetuses with severe health problems that were unlikely to survive past birth.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Brandt, who was pregnant with twins and learned that one of them had a condition in which the skull doesn't fuse properly, traveled to Colorado to abort the nonviable fetus. She said that when she returned to Texas she experienced bloody discharge and was afraid to go to the emergency room because she had obtained a procedure that she was told was illegal in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Another, Samantha Casiano, said she was unable to travel out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when she learned her unborn daughter had a condition in which she wouldn't develop parts of the skull and brain. Casiano said she was afraid to travel out of state because she was worried that she or anyone who helped her could be prosecuted or fined under Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 She carried to term a daughter she named Halo, who was born gasping for air and died within hours.\par \par \uc2 "I just kept telling myself and my baby that, 'I'm so sorry that this had to happen to you,'" she said. "There was no mercy there for her."\par \par \uc2 On cross-examination, Pletscher suggested the women's conditions might have qualified under Texas' existing emergency medical exception. She asked whether any representative of the state had denied them an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to which they all responded that they had only spoken with their medical providers about it.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Adolfo Flores at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:adolfo.flores@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 adolfo.flores@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230719ej7j00669\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
