\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 House GOP Threatens to Derail Defense Bill Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Transgender Care; Democrats have warned that social-issue amendments would be poison pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lindsay Wise, Simon J. Levien and Isaac Yu \par \uc2 1,062 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2023\par 04:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The Republican-controlled House passed contentious measures to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/white-house-lifts-curbs-against-transgender-military-service-members-11611591850"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 transgender care  }}}\uc2  in a series of amendment votes late Thursday, moves that Democrats warned could derail an annual defense-policy bill that has passed Congress on a bipartisan basis for decades.\par \par \uc2 The House approved GOP-backed amendments to the National Defense Authorization Act bill that would restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for service members, ban transgender healthcare for troops and defund diversity initiatives. Measures backed by some conservatives to strike $300 million in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-allies-pledge-air-defenses-for-ukraines-offensive-7da2d218"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 funding to Ukraine  }}}\uc2  and prohibit further security assistance were defeated.\par \par \uc2 The social-policy measures, which passed largely along party lines, are seen as poison pills to many House Democrats, and the amendments' addition to the defense bill could tank its chances of final passage in the narrowly divided House. GOP leaders can lose no more than four Republican votes before needing Democratic support to pass the legislation.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic-led Senate and the Republican-led House are working to pass their own versions of the NDAA, which would authorize $886 billion in spending on national security, including a 5.2% bump in pay for troops\u8212\'20\'14the largest in more than two decades.\par \par \uc2 Rep. Chip Roy (R., Texas), a leading member of the House Freedom Caucus, said the amendments were an effort to undo what he says is Democratic politicization of the military.\par \par \uc2 "The American people I talk to back home don't want a weak military. They don't want a woke military," Roy said. "They don't want to pay for troop sex changes. The American people want a military that can provide for security and win wars."\par \par \uc2 To bring the House's NDAA measure to the floor, Speaker Kevin McCarthy needed votes from members of the hard-line House Freedom Caucus to overcome a procedural hurdle, offering them votes on amendments in return for their support. The rule passed earlier Thursday, and the amendment votes began in the evening.\par \par \uc2 Passing amendments on hot-button issues outraged Democrats, who accused Republicans of injecting culture wars into the NDAA and putting the traditionally bipartisan bill at risk.\par \par \uc2 Rep. \uc2 Joyce Beatty\uc2  (D., Ohio) called the social-issue amendments divisive.\par \par \uc2 "Republicans are choosing MAGA extremism over our military, over women's health rights and over diversity," Beatty said on the House floor.\par \par \uc2 A vote on final passage had been expected Friday, but timing was uncertain as of late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic leadership of the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2  said late Thursday that the changes made to the bill made it unpalatable to them, and they can't pass it as amended. Leaders of the New Democrat Coalition, a group of nearly 100 center-left House Democrats, also announced their opposition, as did House Minority Leader Hakeem Jeffries (D., N.Y.) and his leadership team.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment, which passed 221-213, would prohibit the Secretary of Defense "from paying for or reimbursing expenses relating to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services."\par \par \uc2 Current Pentagon policy allows troops leave and travel funds for reproductive healthcare outside the states where they are stationed, including for abortions or fertility treatment, without being charged time off. U.S. military service members will have access to "lawfully available non-covered reproductive healthcare regardless of where they are stationed," according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.defense.gov/News/Releases/Release/Article/3301006/dod-releases-policies-to-ensure-access-to-non-covered-reproductive-health-care/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pentagon statement  }}}\uc2  announcing the updated policy, which was released earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Many states have tightened \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access after the Supreme Court ruled that there was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 no constitutional right  }}}\uc2  to the procedure. Among the states with the most-restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies that are also home to major military installations are Texas, Louisiana and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Rep. \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2  (D., N.J.), a former \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  helicopter pilot, said that keeping the policy in place is the "bare minimum" the government must do to protect women's healthcare in the military.\par \par \uc2 "Service members signed up to put their lives on the line for our freedoms," Sherrill said on the House floor. "They did not sign up to put their lives on the line or their spouse's lives on the line because they could not get access to basic care."\par \par \uc2 Rep. \uc2 Ronny Jackson\uc2  (R., Texas) defended the amendment, which he sponsored. "The days of the radical left ignoring the law and pushing their social agenda in the military are done. I absolutely will not waver in my defense of the rule of law, therefore, ensuring that taxpayer money does not kill innocent babies."\par \par \uc2 Another amendment that passed 222-211 would ban the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  or Tricare, the military's healthcare program, from providing gender-related surgeries and hormone treatments for transgender people.\par \par \uc2 Since 2021, transgender troops can openly serve in the military, and there currently is support for service members diagnosed with gender dysphoria. Some service members might be eligible for sex reassignment surgery provided by the U.S. military. According to advocacy groups, there are roughly 15,000 transgender service members.\par \par \uc2 Rep. Eli Crane (R., Ariz.), who co-sponsored an amendment that passed prohibiting some discussions of race in \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  training, drew hisses from Democrats when he said his amendment had "nothing to do with whether colored people or Black people or anybody could serve."\par \par \uc2 Beatty, a former chair of the Congressional Black Caucus, demanded that "colored people" be stricken from the record, calling it "offensive and very inappropriate." Crane amended his comments to "people of color," but the House then voted by unanimous consent to strike his words from the record.\par \par \uc2 Once the House and Senate pass their versions of the NDAA, lawmakers will work behind closed doors to reconcile both chambers' bills before sending compromise legislation to the president's desk for his signature later this year.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the pay raise for service members, the House bill would create a Space National Guard, authorize funding to renovate and construct new barracks and to build new combat ships and drones, and establish a special inspector general for Ukraine security assistance to oversee audits of all U.S. aid provided to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lindsay Wise at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:lindsay.wise@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lindsay.wise@wsj.com  }}}\uc2 , Simon J. Levien at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:simon.levien@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 simon.levien@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Isaac Yu at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:isaac.yu@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 isaac.yu@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Isaac Yu at \par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230713ej7d007bx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
