\par \par \uc2 Nelson Amaya Jr., running a National Hispanic Pastors Alliance booth, supports Trump. PHOTO: Alex Leary/The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 "I was enthralled with him," Matthew Hardwick, 58, of Smyrna, Ga., said afterward as the audience broke for lunch. But Trump is still his pick. "If you look at all the heroes in the Bible, only a few were truly righteous," he said. "The lion's share had something wrong with them. God used them to rise above the tide."\par \par \uc2 A number of other attendees said the same\u8212\'20\'14they liked DeSantis but were sticking with Trump.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Which candidate do you think Evangelical voters are most likely to support in the 2024 election? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Pence, the former vice president, questioned Trump's commitment to secure further \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. While he didn't mention the former president by name, he highlighted some of Trump's recent remarks about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics.\par \par \uc2 "Some you will hear from, from this very podium, will say that the Supreme Court returned the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to the states, and nothing should be done at the federal level," Pence said, adding: "Some have even gone on to blame the overturning of Roe versus Wade for election losses in 2022." Pence called for every Republican presidential candidate to "support a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before 15 weeks, as a minimum nationwide standard."\par \par \uc2 Former New Jersey Gov. \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , the candidate in the GOP field most critical of Trump and the only one to directly address him Friday, stressed the importance of character.\par \par \uc2 "Beware of a leader who says that when something goes wrong, it's everybody else's fault," he said. "I'm running because he's let us down. He has let us down because he's unwilling, he's unwilling to take responsibility for any of the mistakes that were made, any of the faults that he has, and any of the things that he's done."\par \par \uc2 After some in the audience booed, Christie responded: "You can boo all you want, but here's the thing, our faith teaches us that people have to take responsibility for what they do."\par \par \uc2 Some other candidates\u8212\'20\'14such as Hutchinson, the former Arkansas governor, Suarez, the Miami mayor, and Scott, the South Carolina senator\u8212\'20\'14got polite or tepid applause.\par \par \uc2 Saturday is the first anniversary of the landmark Supreme Court decision {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/supreme-court-decision-roe-v-wade-6-24-2022?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminating the constitutional right to an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , a top topic in the candidates' speeches. The GOP has been embroiled in debates over how far to push \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions at the state and federal levels. DeSantis this year signed a six-week ban, which Trump has characterized as harsh {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/trump-other-gop-2024-hopefuls-struggle-to-define-abortion-message-71fed983?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 without elaborating on his own position }}}\uc2  beyond pointing to his role in the Supreme Court. Neither man has said whether they would sign a national ban.\par \par \uc2 Nationally, evangelical or born-again Christians have declined as a share of all Americans but accounted for more than 20% of voters in the 2020 elections. They supplied 37% of Trump's total votes, compared with 8% of President Biden's total, according to AP VoteCast, a large survey of the electorate that year.\par \par \uc2 Evangelicals are particularly influential in Iowa and South Carolina, among the first four states on the nomination calendar, and Trump's rivals need to make a strong showing there. DeSantis, who runs second in most polls, has put an emphasis on Iowa, as have Pence and Scott, who most play up their religious credentials.\par \par \uc2 In the 2016 Iowa caucuses, Trump won 22% support among voters who described themselves as born again or evangelical, entrance polls showed, well below the 34% scored among the group by Sen. Ted Cruz of Texas, who narrowly won the caucuses that year. But Trump went on to win the nomination, gaining evangelical loyalty along the way.\par \par \uc2 Roughly a dozen major candidates are challenging Trump for the nomination, despite his strength.\par \par \uc2 Some campaigns privately say Trump's legal problems\u8212\'20\'14he has been indicted twice this year and faces ongoing investigations into the Jan. 6., 2021 Capitol riot and attempts to overturn the election results in Georgia\u8212\'20\'14will continue to wear him down. So far, though, Trump has enjoyed a rallying effect.\par \par \uc2 "I took a look at DeSantis but once Trump got indicted again I went back to him, mostly out of spite for the government," said Chris Williams, 49, of Charlotte, N.C.\par \par \uc2 Trump overcame his personal challenges with religious conservatives in part by picking Pence as his running mate and by making explicit promises, including releasing a list of candidates he would rely on to fill Supreme Court vacancies. He would go on to appoint three to the court, leading to last year's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision.\par \par \uc2 Aaron Zitner contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Alex Leary at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:alex.leary@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 alex.leary@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and John McCormick at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:mccormick.john@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mccormick.john@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230623ej6n0015p\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
