\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 What a Year in Post-Roe America Reveals About  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 ; Support for at least some measure of rights, even in red states, has scrambled political dynamics and divided  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  opponents; Kansas 'was the first alarm bell'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 1,946 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2023\par 09:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tony Lauinger\uc2  spent 50 years fighting to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Oklahoma. When the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  spent 50 years fighting to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Oklahoma. When the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/supreme-court-decision-roe-v-wade-6-24-2022/card/r4CHX2lzaDBIwwiIxYSE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned Roe v. Wade last June  }}}\uc2  and the state criminalized the procedure, the president of Oklahomans for Life saw his once-improbable goal become reality.\par \par \uc2 Six weeks later, voters in neighboring Kansas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-abortion-vote-results-11659440554"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 soundly rejected an amendment to eliminate \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights from that state's constitution  }}}\uc2 . "That was the first alarm bell that went off," said Lauinger, 79 years old. A string of defeats for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents in other states has followed.\par \par \uc2 Nodding to the political climate, Lauinger recently lobbied Oklahoma Republicans to add exceptions for victims of rape and incest to the state's criminal ban, an unusual move for an antiabortion leader. "We believed we needed to learn the lesson that was so clearly before us," he said.\par \par \uc2 Tensions like those in Oklahoma are surfacing in states from the Midwest to the deep South. The Supreme Court's decision a year ago to withdraw constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a pinnacle moment for social conservatives who long dreamed of Roe's demise. Now, they are facing a backlash.\par \par \uc2 As questions about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have shifted more into the political realm, the early post-Roe era has revealed broad support for at least some measure of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, including in some of the nation's reddest states. That has scrambled state political dynamics, boosted Democratic political fortunes and divided \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents on how to react. Pushback against some of the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans also has left a landscape where the availability of the procedure, at least for now, isn't quite as limited as many observers initially anticipated.\par \par \uc2 In the first nine months post-Dobbs, there were some 26,000 fewer abortions in the U.S. within the formal medical system, a decrease of about 3%, according to data from WeCount, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -data project sponsored by the Society of Family Planning, which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. That data doesn't account for an increase in people ordering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from overseas, a legal gray area.\par \par \uc2 In the run-up to the Supreme Court's decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, groups on both sides of the debate expected that roughly half of the states would outlaw many or most abortions if Roe fell. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Today that number stands at 14,  }}}\uc2  after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents hit both political and legal obstacles.\par \par \uc2 "It has absolutely flipped the politics of this," said Steven Greene, a political-science professor at \uc2 North Carolina State University\uc2 . "One of the things we talked about for years is that Republicans cared more about the issue. It seems like you're going to have Democrats caring more about the issue and being more motivated. And that is going to potentially really matter."\par \par \uc2 The Kansas vote last August set the tone. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters then {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-rights-supporters-prevail-in-midterm-ballot-measures-11667986139"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won victories in five more statewide referendums  }}}\uc2  in a politically diverse mix of states, including Michigan and Kentucky. The Michigan vote effectively nullified a state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that had been in force until the Supreme Court issued its Roe decision in 1973.\par \par \uc2 This spring, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/wisconsin-supreme-court-election-shows-abortion-continues-to-sway-voters-4e47297c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wisconsin voters elected a justice  }}}\uc2  who touted her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, an outcome that gave liberals a rare majority on the state's highest court and diminished the chances that a 19th century state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban would be revived.\par \par \uc2 In South Carolina, the state's Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/south-carolinas-6-week-abortion-ban-ruled-unconstitutional-11672940087"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked a six-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban,  }}}\uc2  ruling it violated a state constitutional right to privacy. Florida's highest court is considering whether the state's ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy is lawful, while state lawmakers recently passed a new ban on the procedure after six weeks. Legal challenges across the U.S. continue, leaving clinics in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  battleground states facing uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 "This doesn't happen in a colonoscopy center. You don't have to wait for a judge to rule on something to figure out what you're going to do that day," said Robyn Schickler, chief medical officer at Planned Parenthood of Southwest and Central Florida.\par \par \uc2 The existing bans have had a significant impact, especially for women living in the South. Fewer women are getting abortions in clinics than before the Dobbs decision, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/more-women-requesting-abortion-pills-outside-formal-channels-study-finds-11667316848"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more are ordering pills online to end their pregnancies, outside of formal medical channels  }}}\uc2 . Those who are obtaining the procedure in a clinical setting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/women-encounter-abortion-delays-as-clinics-draw-patients-from-out-of-state-f40e318b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 often are doing so later into pregnancy  }}}\uc2 , because of longer wait times and the challenges and expense of travel.\par \par \uc2 "Obviously, those victories are really critical, and they were hard fought," said Ashley All, who helped lead the Kansas campaign. "But millions of women are without access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even in moments of crisis and trauma. I don't forget the massive devastating loss we had last year."\par \par \uc2 Republican congressional candidates underperformed in the fall midterm elections, winning the U.S. House by a smaller-than-expected margin and failing in their bid to win the Senate. Polls showed that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a top issue that energized Democratic voters, as well as suburban women who identify as Republicans or Independents.\par \par \uc2 Three-quarters of Democrats said in December that they were more likely to vote for a candidate who supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, while less than half of Republicans said they were more likely to vote for one opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to a poll by public-opinion research firm PerryUndem. A Gallup poll released this month showed support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first trimester rising to a record high of 69%. Most Americans remain opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 National antiabortion leaders said they still view the past year as a turning point for their movement. They blamed their losses at the ballot box on being significantly outspent by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups. They said many GOP leaders have failed to effectively advocate for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions because they are anxious about political pushback now that those limits have real-world effects. Previously, it was easier for Republicans to take a strong stand against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  knowing most strict limits were likely to be blocked by the courts.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not the only one leading the prolife movement that is deeply troubled about where Republicans are now," said Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
