\par \par \uc2 Kathaleen Pittman, left, at Hope Medical Group for Women in Shreveport, La., in 2019. PHOTO: Andrea Morales for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Rather than retire or relocate to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -friendly state, Pittman is hoping to keep her clinic in the South, still close enough to serve women from the same communities, who often were low-income and unable or unlikely to fly somewhere else.\par \par \uc2 Pittman, who hopes to open the new location this summer, is doing everything she can to keep costs down while the situation in the region remains uncertain. She lives with a roommate for the first time since college and initially plans to offer only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills at the clinic, not surgical procedures.\par \par \uc2 She hopes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups can pull off more victories and expand access in Republican-led states in the area. "I truly believe that maybe the Republicans have gone too far this time around," she said.\par \par \uc2 Alex Leary and Jennifer Calfas contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230623ej6n000rz\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
