\par \par \uc2 Nebraska Sen. Machaela Cavanaugh led the filibuster movement against the bill and said she would continue to filibuster into 2024. PHOTO: Rebecca S. Gratz for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Opponents of the bill have fought the attempts to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gender-affirming care throughout the session. They argued the efforts defy recommendations from medical organizations and professionals, and have called their opponents hypocritical for backing bills the liberal lawmakers say limit individual freedom.\par \par \uc2 "These are the same people who claim to be defenders of freedom and liberty, and yet they seek to deny transgender individuals the ability to live and be free and be themselves as their true selves," said Sen. Megan Hunt, whose son is transgender and who has used the filibuster to delay bills with Cavanaugh this session. "That is not freedom. That's not liberty. That's not morality."\par \par \uc2 The Nebraska bill banning gender-affirming procedures for Nebraskans under 19 permits children and teens taking puberty blockers or hormone therapies before the law would take effect to continue those treatments. It gives the state's chief medical officer\u8212\'20\'14who is appointed by the governor\u8212\'20\'14the power to regulate gender-affirming care, including hormone treatments, that doesn't involve surgery.\par \par \uc2 The legislation would also ban most abortions after 12 weeks of pregnancy, with exceptions for rape, incest and in life-threatening medical emergencies. Republican-affiliated members of the Legislature said the 12-week ban is a compromise. The proposed ban on most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy fell one vote short of moving forward last month.\par \par \uc2 Sandy Danek, executive director of Nebraska Right to Life, said the organization was "extremely disappointed" the six-week ban failed to pass, but appreciated lawmakers' efforts to advance an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restriction this session. Under the 12-week ban, she said, "there are still so many lives that will be lost."\par \par \uc2 More than 85% of abortions in Nebraska in 2021 occurred before 12 weeks of pregnancy, according to state health-department data. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans implemented in more than a dozen states across the country have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/women-encounter-abortion-delays-as-clinics-draw-patients-from-out-of-state-f40e318b?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pushed some abortions later into pregnancy }}}\uc2  because of increased demand and delays at clinics in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal.\par \par \uc2 The law is "going to have a more detrimental effect on people trying to access care because of this network of care that has been so impacted across the country," Andi Curry Grubb, executive director of Planned Parenthood Advocates of Nebraska, said before the Legislature's final vote.\par \par \uc2 Cavanaugh, who led the filibuster movement against the bill, said she would continue to filibuster into 2024.\par \par \uc2 "The people will remember today," she said before debate officially began Friday. "And they will remember that you did not act with kindness, you did not act with compassion, you did not take into consideration your role and responsibility to them."\par \par \uc2 Write to Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jennifer.calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jennifer.calfas@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230519ej5j003s5\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
