\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Antiabortion Group Used Cellphone Data to Target Ads to Planned Parenthood Visitors; Wisconsin group used precise geolocation data until last year to direct ads to women it suspected of seeking abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Byron Tau in Washington and Patience Haggin in New York \par \uc2 1,569 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2023\par 13:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A Midwest antiabortion group used cellphone location data to target online content to visitors of certain Planned Parenthood clinics, according to people familiar with the matter and documents reviewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 "Took the first pill at the clinic? It may not be too late to save your pregnancy," reads one ad such visitors were served on social-media sites including \uc2 Facebook\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Veritas Society, a nonprofit fund established by the organization Wisconsin Right to Life, was using precise geolocation data to target those ads from as early as November 2019 through late last year, according to a Veritas Society website, several former employees of an advertising-technology company it used to target the ads, and other people familiar with the matter. The ad company told Veritas Society it had to stop because it was violating the company's rules about targeting sensitive locations, the former employees said.\par \par \uc2 The campaign used a common digital-advertising technology called geofencing to extract the unique device identifiers of phones carried into Planned Parenthood and other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, the people said. It then used those device IDs to target those people on popular social-networking sites such as \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  with antiabortion messaging. All three social networks said the ads violated their policies and said future such campaigns would be rejected.\par \par \uc2 When users clicked on the targeted ads, they were directed to a site registered to Veritas Society that gave them two options: "I want to undo the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill" or "I am thinking about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill." That site could track users who answered and target ads to them as they moved around the web.\par \par \uc2 The executive director and other officers of both Veritas Society and Wisconsin Right to Life didn't respond to multiple requests for comment. A spokeswoman for Planned Parenthood called the ads Veritas Society was running "disinformation," saying: "Planned Parenthood is committed to providing sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and information in settings which preserve and protect the essential privacy and rights of everyone."\par \par \uc2 Snap said the ads violated multiple Snap policies, including one prohibiting advertisers from using sensitive information to target ads on \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 . \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 's parent company, \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2 , said through a spokesman the ads were improperly labeled as nonpolitical, when all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  content had to be identified as a political ad.\par \par \uc2 Using location data linked to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics to target advertising and extract device IDs hasn't been found to break any federal laws, and a judge rejected a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ftc-sues-idaho-company-for-selling-sensitive-tracking-data-11661798303"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 civil suit brought by federal regulators }}}\uc2  that sought to crack down on the sale of such data that could be used to track visits to sensitive locations. But the campaign shows how advertising companies\u8212\'20\'14and any group that hires them\u8212\'20\'14can mine even the most intimate details of people's lives in ways consumers are often not aware of.\par \par \uc2 The possible consequences of such tracking have grown since a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court ruling last year }}}\uc2  created a patchwork of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations across the country, with some states now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banning the procedure outright }}}\uc2 . Opponents of the court's ruling and privacy advocates say personal \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive-health\b0 \highlight\uc2  data could be publicized or used to build a legal case against people seeking or providing abortions.\par \par \uc2 The aim of the campaign was to educate women about alternatives to terminating a pregnancy, according to a Veritas Society website that was online until late last year.\par \par \uc2 On that site, Veritas Society said it could identify phones on the premises of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and extract their device IDs\u8212\'20\'14the unique identifiers given by \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  to mobile devices to help advertisers target consumers across phone apps and websites.\par \par \uc2 With such IDs and some basic analysis, it is sometimes possible to link a phone to a real-world address, and potentially a name, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/law-enforcements-use-of-commercial-phone-data-stirs-surveillance-fight-11631707201?mod=djemCybersecruityPro&tpl=cy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Journal has previously reported. }}}\uc2  It couldn't be learned whether Veritas Society took that step.\par \par \uc2 "We captured the cell phone IDs of women who visited all Planned Parenthood locations in Wisconsin along with similar locations and their associated parking areas," the Veritas Society website said. The documents also depicted Planned Parenthood locations in Montclair, N.J., Fresno, Calif., Tallahassee, Fla., and Colorado Springs, Colo., as having been geofenced. An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://web.archive.org/web/20220831201929/https://veritassociety.com/digital-technology/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 archived version remains online. }}}\par \par \uc2 Location data is usually collected when consumers allow an app such as a game or weather service to know their precise GPS location. The app then permits advertisers to serve ads to the consumer based on their location history. Device IDs such as those used by Veritas Society are designed to be shared with advertisers, but many advertising platforms have policies against geofencing sensitive locations such as healthcare facilities.\par \par \uc2 Veritas Society sought to build a nationwide campaign around geofencing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics\par \par \uc2 Digital advertising is a half-trillion-dollar industry in which companies use consumers' behavior both online and offline to build richly detailed profiles that sometimes contain highly intimate material about real individuals. Many companies that operate digital-advertising services lack the ability to consistently police the ads that run on their platform or to audit how their data is being used. In most cases, there is no law requiring them to do so.\par \par \uc2 Veritas Society ran an initial campaign in Wisconsin in late 2019 and 2020, according to the archived website. The nonprofit was raising money for a nationwide campaign as recently as last year and seeking other antiabortion groups as partners, according to the website. Veritas Society also geofenced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and bought ads in Arkansas, according to people familiar with the campaign.\par \par \uc2 Former employees at the advertising-technology and location-data company \uc2 Near Intelligence\uc2  said a digital-ad agency working on behalf of Veritas Society was extracting data about devices visiting Planned Parenthoods as late as last year from Near's platform. Near has policies against targeting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, the former employees said.\par \par \uc2 "Near continually strives to maintain strict confidentiality of customer information and adheres to robust privacy and data protection policies, ensuring that sensitive data is not shared or used to target vulnerable communities or identify individuals in sensitive areas. Near exclusively collaborates with trusted, authorized, and experienced advertising partners to prevent any unauthorized use of our data," said a spokesman for the company.\par \par \uc2 Other people familiar with the organization's campaign said Veritas Society placed ads on popular social-media platforms, as described in the organization's own documents. The ads touted "\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversal" and linked to websites promoting the use of the hormone progesterone to reverse medication abortions, a procedure the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  has said is "not supported by science."\par \par \uc2 The Veritas Society said on its website that its geofencing-based campaign in Wisconsin served 14.3 million ad impressions in late 2019 and 2020 across mobile devices that had been carried into \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. It served ads to those devices across \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 , which included links to the Veritas Society website. "This led to 48,300 visits to our website. Of those 48,300 visitors, 11,463 returned for a second visit to obtain additional pro-life messages and information on where to go for pregnancy assistance," the society said.\par \par \uc2 Regulators have challenged the use of sensitive geolocation data\par \par \uc2 Near, the location broker whose data was used to geofence the Planned Parenthood clinics and extract the mobile-phone data, was one among a number of similar companies that faced inquiries from regulators last year over whether its data set could be used for tracking people seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Should antiabortion groups be able to access cellphone data of private individuals? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 The company told investors it received an inquiry from members of the House of Representatives about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracking in July. "We communicated to the Members that the company doesn't allow the use of its data for law enforcement or healthcare purposes, including the disclosure of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  information," Near said in a filing with the \uc2 U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While no federal law prohibits targeting ads to visitors at \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, some states have passed privacy laws that restrict the use of sensitive data, including health information and geolocation. The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  alleged in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ftc-sues-idaho-company-for-selling-sensitive-tracking-data-11661798303"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a recent lawsuit }}}\uc2 , later dismissed, that selling mobile-phone data on consumers' movements violated federal consumer-protection laws. The commission expressed concern about the sale of data relating to "locations associated with medical care, \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , religious worship, mental health, temporary shelters, such as shelters for the homeless, domestic violence survivors, or other at-risk populations, and addiction recovery."\par \par \uc2 After Veritas Society learned it could no longer geofence \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, the fund's parent organization, Wisconsin Right to Life, asked its partner ad agency whether bars and clubs could be targeted as another potential way to reach sexually active young women, according to Steven Bogue, managing partner of Recrue Media, who was involved in placing the original ads. Together, they determined that it was more effective to target people who searched for information on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than to geofence such locations, Bogue said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Byron Tau at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:byron.tau@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 byron.tau@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Patience Haggin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:patience.haggin@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 patience.haggin@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230518ej5i002mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
