\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 State Lawmakers Take Aim at Voter-Led Ballot Measures; In some GOP-led states, legislators want to make it harder to amend constitutions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shannon Najmabadi and Kris Maher \par \uc2 1,147 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2023\par 13:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lawmakers in Republican-led states are backing measures to make it harder for voters to amend state constitutions, as partisan fights play out over {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access }}}\uc2  and other issues.\par \par \uc2 Momentum behind such bills has reached new levels since the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  last June. Lawmakers this year have been explicit in making the ballot-initiative issue a proxy war on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Since the court's ruling, voters in at least six states including California, Michigan and Vermont have effectively bypassed state legislatures\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-rights-supporters-prevail-in-midterm-ballot-measures-11667986139"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by voting to add \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights }}}\uc2  to state constitutions or to reject \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in states such as Missouri and Ohio are planning to put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related measures on the ballot as soon as this fall.\par \par \uc2 Republican supporters say the measures to curtail ballot initiatives are needed to keep moneyed special interest groups from loading up constitutions with issues that should be handled legislatively, from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gambling and marijuana. The proposed bills typically increase the level of support an initiative needs to pass from a simple majority to 60% of the vote.\par \par \uc2 "The Ohio constitution is supposed to serve as a framework of our state government, not as a tool for special interests," said Ohio Secretary of State \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2 , who supports one such measure.\par \par \uc2 Critics of the proposals say they undercut voters' ability to make their voices heard and strip a method by which residents in several GOP-led states have approved Democratic priorities in recent years.\par \par \uc2 More than 160 proposals affecting the ballot initiative process were introduced in 35 states this year, according to a tally from the Ballot Initiative Strategy Center, an organization that supports progressive ballot measures.\par \par \uc2 "It was really when we started to see these quote-unquote progressive issues start to win at the ballot that we started to see these attacks," said Chris Melody Fields Figueredo, the Ballot Initiative Strategy Center's executive director.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, Republican lawmakers have backed a measure to require a 60% majority to approve any attempt to amend the constitution. On Wednesday, after a debate during which protesters filled the capitol, the GOP-led House passed a separate resolution to hold a special election this August in which voters will decide whether to approve the new threshold.\par \par \uc2 A Missouri bill would have required a 57% majority to approve constitutional amendments. The measure failed to pass the Senate in an end-of-session filibuster, but the legislation's sponsor vowed to bring it back up.\par \par \uc2 "It takes three-fourths of the states to ratify an amendment to change the U.S. Constitution," said Republican state Rep. Mike Henderson, who sponsored the bill. "I believe our Constitution should be a living document but that it shouldn't be an ever-growing document."\par \par \uc2 Two dozen states have initiative processes, which involve voters collecting a certain number of signatures to place a proposed law or constitutional amendment on the ballot. Some states require that lawmakers consider the proposals before they are voted on by residents.\par \par \uc2 Florida already requires a 60% vote to approve a change to a constitutional amendment. Arizona requires a 60% vote for tax measures, and Colorado has a 55% threshold for some issues.\par \par \uc2 A recent Arkansas law raised from 15 to 50 the number of counties where a minimum number of signatures must be gathered from to get an initiative on the ballot. The law has been challenged in court by a Republican state senator and the League of Women Voters of Arkansas. Arkansas voters previously rejected a 2022 effort to require a 60% vote to approve ballot initiatives, and a 2020 measure that would have required signatures from 45 counties.\par \par \uc2 Democrats and left-leaning groups in some states say the gap between the legislature and voters on issues such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can partly be traced to years of gerrymandering, which has made state legislatures more conservative than their voters overall.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, nearly three-quarters of Ohio voters passed a ballot proposal to require bipartisan cooperation in redrawing congressional and legislative maps. Last year, the state Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ohio-supreme-court-strikes-down-new-congressional-map-as-favoring-gop-11642195869"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ordered the legislature to redraw maps }}}\uc2  made after 2018 because it said that they gave Republicans an unfair advantage.\par \par \uc2 Kellie Copeland, executive director of Pro-Choice Ohio, said she believes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters will gather more than 400,000 signatures by early July in order to put a measure on the November ballot to add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections to the state constitution. She said Republican state lawmakers "know they don't have the majority support of Ohioans for their agenda."\par \par \uc2 Mike Gonidakis, president of Ohio Right to Life, said a range of groups believe issues such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , raising the minimum wage and legalizing recreational marijuana should be handled legislatively.\par \par \uc2 "This isn't just pro-lifers and social conservatives," he said. "Those are all policy decisions that should be in the Ohio revised code, not our state constitution where basic fundamental rights should be."\par \par \uc2 Other critics of the legislation say it fits into a recent push to restrict access to the ballot.\par \par \uc2 "It's important to situate the attacks on the ballot measure process as part of the broader set of attacks on voting rights and democracy writ large," said Kelly Hall, executive director of the Fairness Project, a nonprofit that has backed successful ballot initiatives to raise the minimum wage and expand Medicaid, among other issues.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Should there be limits to what voter-led ballot measures can achieve? Why or why not? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Political and social science professors said that ballot initiatives' increased prominence reflects dissatisfaction with elected officials that has grown in the last 15 years.\par \par \uc2 "Both parties are unable to pass legislation to deal with America's problems," said Michael McQuarrie, an \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2  professor who directs the Center for Work and Democracy there. So "ballot initiatives all of a sudden get really, really important."\par \par \uc2 Joshua Dyck, professor and chair of the political science department at \uc2 University of Massachusetts Lowell\uc2 , said conservatives have in the past successfully used ballot initiatives themselves. More than two dozen states passed initiatives banning same-sex marriage before a 2015 \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  ruling legalized the practice nationwide. Multiple antitax measures were pushed by conservatives\u8212\'20\'14and approved\u8212\'20\'14in the 1970s and 1980s.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Dyck co-wrote a book arguing that ballot initiatives mainly encourage greater conflict among voters, rather than produce political and social benefits. He supports raising the bar to use them. Supporters these days, he says, are "only thinking of what this means for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in the next two years."\par \par \uc2 Write to Shannon Najmabadi at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:shannon.najmabadi@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shannon.najmabadi@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Kris Maher at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Kris.Maher@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kris.Maher@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230515ej5f0015q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
