\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Liberal Prosecutors Tussle With State Officials Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Drug Crimes; Appeals court will hear arguments on Florida governor's suspension of Tampa prosecutor\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto and Mariah Timms \par \uc2 1,052 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2023\par 21:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A federal appeals court on Tuesday heard arguments over whether {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/florida-ron-desantis-suspends-prosecutor-andrew-warren-abortion-gender-transition-cases-11659629739"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Tampa-area prosecutor who was suspended }}}\uc2  by Florida Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  should get his job back, the most high-profile example of the growing confrontations between state officials and local prosecutors who decline to enforce certain laws.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, dozens of liberal local prosecutors {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/state-abortion-restrictions-loom-some-officials-promise-not-to-enforce-them-11653235200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have made promises not to enforce bans on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  or certain low-level drug offenses, which they say are harmful to their communities and a poor use of limited resources.\par \par \uc2 Governors and legislatures have said these decisions are lawless and have moved to give statewide officials more power to remove local prosecutors or take over certain prosecutions.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors have long been able to exercise discretion to focus their offices' resources on pursuing the worst crimes and to ignore arcane laws. And state officials have longstanding authority to step in if a prosecutor isn't fulfilling their role or is bungling a key case.\par \par \uc2 Law professors say these conflicts have grown more acute with a recent wave of elected prosecutors who have been vocal on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/florida-gov-ron-desantis-signs-15-week-abortion-ban-into-law-11649961049"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hot-button issues including \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  and policing.\par \par \uc2 "As criminal justice politics have gotten more conflicted there are incentives for prosecutors to be more overt about not enforcing laws," said Zachary Price, a professor at the University of California College of Law, San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 One catalyst for some of the recent clashes was a statement organized by Fair and Just Prosecution, a nonprofit network of liberal prosecutors. The statement, released shortly after the Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned Roe v. Wade last June }}}\uc2 , was signed by more than 80 prosecutors who said they wouldn't focus their offices' resources on enforcing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 "We stand together in our firm belief that prosecutors have a responsibility to refrain from using limited criminal legal system resources to criminalize personal medical decisions," the prosecutors said.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Warren, the Democratic Florida state attorney for Hillsborough County, was one of the signatories.\par \par \uc2 In August, Mr. DeSantis, a Republican, announced Mr. Warren's suspension at a press conference. The night before, his then-press secretary tweeted, "Prepare for the liberal media meltdown of the year." Mr. Warren was escorted from his office by an armed law enforcement officer.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis tapped a state judge, Susan Lopez, to step in as a replacement.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Warren {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/suspended-florida-prosecutor-andrew-warren-sues-governor-ron-desantis-to-be-reinstated-11660760861"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued Mr. DeSantis in federal court }}}\uc2 , calling the dismissal an illegal attempt by the governor "to silence his critics, promote his loyalists, and subvert the will of the voters."\par \par \uc2 In court filings, Mr. DeSantis said Mr. Warren's public statements on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other issues would "invite lawlessness" and reflected Mr. Warren's "fundamental misunderstanding of his role as a Florida prosecutor."\par \par \uc2 The governor's view received support from some 15 Republican attorneys general who wrote in an amicus brief, "Governors can protect their States from the constitutional dangers\u8212\'20\'14not to mention the physical dangers\u8212\'20\'14posed by prosecutors who refuse to enforce legislatively enacted criminal prohibitions."\par \par \uc2 A U.S. district judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/judge-says-desantis-violated-laws-in-suspending-prosecutor-but-does-not-reinstate-him-11674248326"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled the firing violated the First Amendment }}}\uc2  because Mr. Warren was removed for his political views. He also said the dismissal was improper under the Florida Constitution because Mr. DeSantis failed to show Mr. Warren had neglected his duties. But Judge Robert Hinkle, a Clinton appointee, said he didn't have the power as a federal judge to order Mr. Warren reinstated. Mr. Warren appealed to the Atlanta-based 11th U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals, which heard arguments Tuesday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The appeals panel appeared reluctant to second-guess the trial judge's findings in a case that produced volumes of evidence from both sides. The panel questioned Mr. Warren's attorney at length about whether the governor had suspended the prosecutor based on his beliefs or because of his conduct as a state attorney.\par \par \uc2 "This governor punishes dissenting voices. It's what he does. It's what he did to Mr. Warren. It violates the First Amendment," said David O'Neil, Mr. Warren's attorney.\par \par \uc2 Florida Solicitor General Henry Whitaker said the governor's primary motivation was Mr. Warren's decision not to prosecute certain crimes\par \par \uc2 "An elected official might think that his constituents would approve of him suspending a prosecutor that the governor thought was lawless," Mr. Whitaker said.\par \par \uc2 Similar conflicts are playing out in other states. Lawmakers in Georgia recently passed a bill creating a state-appointed oversight board that has the power to discipline and remove elected prosecutors. Supporters say it is intended to hold district attorneys accountable for decisions not to prosecute categories of crimes, or for unprofessional conduct.\par \par \uc2 Fulton County District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 , the prosecutor in Atlanta who is investigating former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/charging-decisions-in-trump-probe-will-come-in-summer-atlanta-prosecutor-fani-willis-says-8a830d9b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 possible election interference }}}\uc2 , has criticized the push as improperly overturning the will of the voters.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Should district attorneys be required to enforce laws that criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Why or why not? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 "This bill is dangerous. It is dangerous to undo the voters because you don't like someone and you don't like their policies and you don't like what they do," she told the Georgia State Senate Judiciary Committee at a tense hearing in February.\par \par \uc2 She went on to note that the bill was only filed after the number of nonwhite district attorneys in the state nearly tripled since elections in 2020. Ms. Willis and several senators debated whether the impetus for the bill was racist, and whether mentioning that she believed it to be so was in itself offensive.\par \par \uc2 Legislators in states including Indiana and Texas have considered similar bills this session.\par \par \uc2 In Missouri, lawmakers are advancing a bill that would allow the governor to appoint a special prosecutor in any jurisdiction where the homicide rate exceeds 35 cases per every 100,000 people. That is currently the situation in St. Louis, where the state's Republican attorney general earlier this year initiated court proceedings to remove the elected circuit attorney, a Democrat, from office on allegations she and her office have repeatedly neglected their duties.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Mariah Timms at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:mariah.timms@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mariah.timms@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230502ej52003jt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
