\par\par \uc2 Politics & Ideas\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The GOP Is Out of Step on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 ; More-restrictive bans might play well in the primaries but are a loser in November.\b0\par\par\uc2 By William A. Galston \par \uc2 842 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2023\par 16:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As the midterm election results rolled in this past November, it soon became clear that Republicans were failing to achieve the large gains that many had predicted. Postelection analyses showed that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was one of the main reasons for this surprising result. The Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe v. Wade, which had been the law of the land for nearly half a century, came as a shock to the many Americans who had taken access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for granted, and who then went to the polls in large numbers to express their dismay. The sting of loss was felt more intensely than the pleasure of gain: Those who were disturbed by the decision rated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as more important in determining their vote than did those who supported the court's decision.\par \par \uc2 Republicans who hoped the issue would fade have been disappointed. State ballot measures and elections have kept the controversy alive, and the new controversy over the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication mifepristone has turned up the heat. A recent Yahoo News/\uc2 YouGov\uc2  poll {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.yahoo.com/poll-most-americans-say-supreme-court-should-reverse-texas-ruling-on-abortion-pill-mifepristone-142912238.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shows}}}\uc2  that a majority of Americans favor continued access to this drug. It also finds that "Americans who say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a very important voting issue" think Democrats do a "better job" addressing it than Republicans by a margin of 52% to 27%. The Republican Party has taken the minority position on an election-determining issue and is divided about how to respond.\par \par \uc2 As a matter of law, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization ended the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and returned the issue to the states. The most likely outcome\u8212\'20\'14a patchwork of practices, some restrictive, others permissive\u8212\'20\'14did not satisfy antiabortion activists, who began agitating for a national ban.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Dannenfelser declared that "life is a matter of human rights, not states' rights" and promised that her group, \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, would oppose any presidential candidate who failed to endorse at least a 15-week federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Most Republican voters disagree. Another \uc2 YouGov{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.cdn.yougov.com/6jir2a8rbh/econTabReport.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2 , conducted April 15-18 for the Economist, found only 34% of Republicans favor deciding the issue nationally, while 52% prefer to leave it to the states.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy seems likely to become a litmus test for their presidential candidates. As of now, only former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  has indicated interest in a national ban, while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who reportedly regards \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a losing issue for his party, has avoided addressing that question directly. Florida Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , currently Mr. Trump's principal rival for the Republican nomination, pushed successfully for a six-week ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his state, with exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the mother. But Mr. DeSantis signed the bill with minimal publicity and told GOP activists in New Hampshire that what works in Florida may not elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis's law may appeal to Republican primary voters, who supported it 68% to 27% in a recent Wall Street Journal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/public/resources/documents/WSJ_Poll_April_2023_redacted.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poll}}}\uc2 . But what works in the primaries might not in the general election: 53% of the electorate opposes the terms of Florida's law while only 41% supports them. Nearly 6 in 10 Americans believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, a position endorsed by only 30% of Republicans. Mr. DeSantis and the GOP are out of step with the country.\par \par \uc2 The boxer Joe Louis once remarked of an elusive opponent, "He can run but he can't hide." Nor can Republican candidates, who must seriously defend what they advocate. It is risky to treat a grave moral question as a political calculation.\par \par \uc2 This may be a problem for Mr. Trump, who has demonstrated that he prizes victory over everything. But my guess is that he can solve this problem by doing what he does best\u8212\'20\'14going on the offensive. When the issue comes up during the first Republican presidential debate, he can remind the audience that if it weren't for him, Roe would still be the law of the land. You can imagine the quote: "For decades, other Republicans just talked about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Only I delivered. Without me, we wouldn't even be talking about what to do next."\par \par \uc2 He could then play the statesman. "Unlike the Democrats, our party loves freedom. We saw what happened during the Covid pandemic when the Democrats forced people to do things they didn't accept. We can't make the same mistake. To move forward, we have to persuade people that we're right, and we have to treat them with compassion and respect."\par \par \uc2 Far-fetched? Perhaps. But after a shaky start, Mr. Trump is running a disciplined and effective campaign. He knows he can't turn \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into a winning issue. And his lack of a strong moral position gives him a chance to win the nomination in a way that mitigates the damage to his general-election prospects.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230425ej4p003e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
