\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 DeSantis's Gamble on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 ; A Florida ban after six weeks will echo through the 2024 campaign.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 737 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2023\par 22:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 's decision to sign a new Florida law that bans most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy is a political gamble that Democrats are eager to attack. The Governor's obligation now is to explain and defend it if he wants to win the White House.\par \par \uc2 Though Democrats will never admit it, the current \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate vindicates Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 's majority opinion in Dobbs last year overruling Roe v. Wade. "The Constitution does not prohibit the citizens of each State from regulating or prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he wrote. "Roe and Casey arrogated that authority. We now overrule those decisions and return that authority to the people and their elected representatives."\par \par \uc2 He added earlier in his opinion that "we do not pretend to know how our political system or society will respond to today's decision." Democrats predicted doom, but so far they are winning the post-Dobbs political debate in most places. The Court's ruling was silent on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, and states as expected have gone in different directions.\par \par \uc2 The Florida law will now be one of the country's most restrictive. The state had a 15-week ban, but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents deemed that too expansive since more than 90% of abortions occur before 15 weeks. But the new law amounts to a near-ban because at six weeks many women may not know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 The law has restrictions for rape, incest and human-trafficking pregnancies up to 15 weeks. It also has an exception if the life of the mother is endangered. Drugs that induce abortions would have to be dispensed in person, not by mail. Democrats will portray this as a blanket ban, and every poll of public opinion says voters oppose a ban even as they support some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Florida Republicans deserve to be taken at their word that their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a matter of moral conviction. The bill passed the Florida House 70-40, so it wasn't close. Mr. DeSantis could have tried to maneuver his Legislature to make the law less restrictive, perhaps by lowering the 15-week ban to 10. He didn't, so presumably he also supports the ban out of personal belief. This is admirable in our age of political cynicism.\par \par \uc2 But if Mr. DeSantis runs for President, he will have to defend the law on the national stage. He will thus be presented with the challenge facing any political figure who believes in the morality of a cause that is unpopular.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis may figure the six-week ban will help him in the GOP primaries against other candidates competing for the votes of social conservatives. He might be right. But GOP donor Thomas Peterffy has said his support for Mr. DeSantis is on hold because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law and other social positions.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has also been notably silent about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Dobbs, and his advisers are telling reporters that Mr. Trump thinks the issue is a loser for Republicans. The aides are also quietly spinning that Florida's six-week ban makes Mr. DeSantis less electable in the general election against a Democrat. Mr. Trump may figure that since he appointed the Justices who overturned Roe, he can now run to the left of Mr. DeSantis on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Like Justice Alito, we don't pretend to know how this debate will turn out in 2024. Georgia Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  signed a six-week ban after Dobbs and easily won re-election last year. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is merely one issue among many that voters will consider as they measure the candidates. But one certainty is that Mr. DeSantis will be asked about the six-week ban again and again. The press is largely pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and believes the issue will hurt Republicans, so it will come up in every debate.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis could say the issue should be settled at the state level, and that Florida's law shouldn't dictate to Wisconsin or Pennsylvania. That works for us, but you can expect other candidates to challenge that as insufficient. Mr. DeSantis will have to defend the six-week ban in any case. Now that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  die is cast, Mr. DeSantis's presidential chances may hang on how well he defends the law just signed.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230416ej4g0035x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
