FDED708FBBDCE74EF7BADBFDEE78CFBBDEF7CEF7BEFBFDEF800FBCE0074FF8C21BFEF0884FBCE217CF78B40704003B}}
\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 A Split Decision on the  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill; The mifepristone case seems destined for the Supreme Court.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 474 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2023\par 22:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals late Wednesday stayed a lower court order suspending the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's approval of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone while keeping in place an injunction on the agency's relaxations of dispensing rules. Call it a split decision that seems destined for the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 The three-judge panel's order, on a 2-1 ruling, gave both anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  physicians and the government some of the relief they requested. On one hand, the panel held that pro-life physicians' challenge to the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of mifepristone in 2000 might be barred by a six-year statute of limitation. Plaintiffs claimed the \uc2 FDA\uc2  "reopened" its 2000 approval in 2021 when it rejected their petition for review, but the panel said this isn't clear.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the panel largely agreed with federal Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk that the agency's 2016 and 2021 relaxations of its safety precautions were arbitrary and capricious. The approval in 2000 required the supervision of a "qualified physician" and three in-person office visits to administer the drug regimen and monitor potential complications.\par \par \uc2 In 2016 the \uc2 FDA\uc2  increased the maximum gestational age at which a woman could receive the drug from 49 to 70 days; reduced the number of required in-person office visits from three to one; and allowed non-doctors to prescribe and administer it. In 2021 the agency eliminated the in-person dispensing requirement so women could receive the pill by mail.\par \par \uc2 The panel says the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  physicians have standing to sue because some have treated women with complications after taking the pill and have had to surgically complete abortions against their conscience. The appellate panel said the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's easing of its dispensing rules increased the risk of complications and the likelihood that doctors would have to treat them.\par \par \uc2 As for the merits, the panel disagreed with how the \uc2 FDA\uc2  analyzed evidence to support easing its dispensing regulations. While some of the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's judgments do seem questionable, this could be said about many of its drug approval decisions. Courts can strike down agency decisions that don't follow proper procedure or exceed its statutory authority. But the \uc2 FDA\uc2  clearly has authority over drug approvals, and courts lack the technical expertise to second-guess the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's scientific assessments.\par \par \uc2 The appellate ruling reinstates the status quo before 2016 when access to the pill was widespread but more restricted than in recent years. Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  says the Administration will appeal to the Supreme Court, and it's hard to predict how the Justices will come out. One thing is certain: Democrats are delighted that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics will stay at the top of the news.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230413ej4d006mx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
