\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 A Legal Clash Over the  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill; Many on the right and left want courts to set national  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  policy again.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 763 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2023\par 21:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a political gift to Democrats that they want to keep giving, which explains why they sounded almost gleefully furious on Friday after a Texas federal judge overruled the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's approval of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone. A conflicting order by another lower court issued the same day means the issue could be headed to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 The Court's Dobbs decision last summer handed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation to the states, but many on the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights left are trying to nationalize it again via the courts. This is the important context for the donnybrook over the abortifacient mifepristone, which the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has authorized through the first 10 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Pro-life doctors last year challenged the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's 2000 mifepristone approval and later agency actions that eased regulations on how it is dispensed. They argue in Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine v. \uc2 FDA\uc2  that the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's decisions were arbitrary and capricious under the Administrative Procedure Act (APA).\par \par \uc2 Federal Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk agreed but made several legal leaps. He blows past the plaintiffs' failure to show a concrete and particular injury from the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's actions, which is a threshold requirement to sue in federal court. If there is no injury in fact, there is no case or controversy for courts to adjudicate.\par \par \uc2 Plaintiffs claim that patients who suffer alleged adverse events from the drug could overwhelm the medical system and stress doctors. They also could potentially face increased medical malpractice and insurance costs if they had to treat more patients who suffered adverse events. But these harms are speculative.\par \par \uc2 There is also a six-year statute of limitations to challenge \uc2 FDA\uc2  actions in court. The window to contest the 2000 approval closed long ago. Judge Kacsmaryk ruled that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  reopened it when it eased regulations on the drug's dispensing in 2021. But this is a legal stretch.\par \par \uc2 As for the merits, the plaintiffs have a point that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  stretched its regulations to fast-track the drug under its accelerated approval pathway "for serious or life-threatening illnesses." Pregnancy is neither. But plaintiffs cherry-pick evidence on the drug's safety and ask the judge to second-guess \uc2 FDA\uc2  judgments, which isn't the role of the courts.\par \par \uc2 The judiciary's responsibility under the APA is to ensure the "agency has acted within a zone of reasonableness," as the Supreme Court explained in 2021. A judge may only set aside an agency action if isn't supported by the record, ignores important facts or questions, or isn't reasonably explained. The plaintiffs don't convincingly prove any of these.\par \par \uc2 Judge Kacsmaryk says the "\uc2 FDA\uc2  'entirely failed to consider an important aspect of the problem' by omitting any evaluation of the psychological effects of the drug or an evaluation of the long-term medical consequences of the drug." But the \uc2 FDA\uc2  doesn't consider these factors for other drugs it approves, and it imposed safety precautions on its use.\par \par \uc2 Not coincidentally, these \uc2 FDA\uc2  precautions were also recently challenged under the APA by 17 Democratic state attorneys general (State of Washington v. \uc2 FDA\uc2 ). They claim the drug is "far safer than continuing a pregnancy," and that healthcare professionals should be able to prescribe it and pharmacies dispense it without safety precautions.\par \par \uc2 Their lawsuit is equally flawed. The states haven't suffered a concrete harm from the \uc2 FDA\uc2  safety precautions nor exhausted the agency's administrative process as required before bringing a lawsuit. Like the pro-life groups, they second-guess \uc2 FDA\uc2  judgments and make highly questionable claims that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill is safer than over-the-counter drugs like Tylenol.\par \par \uc2 Federal Judge Thomas Rice nonetheless agreed with the \uc2 AGs\uc2  and on Friday enjoined the \uc2 FDA\uc2  from reducing mifepristone's availability in their states. This conflicts with Judge Kacsmaryk's nationwide injunction on its approval. The lower-court clash means the High Court could soon be asked to intervene.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The Justices in Dobbs sought to extricate themselves from regulating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but partisans on both sides don't want to let them. The partisans don't appreciate that the stakes in these two cases go beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If pro-life groups or Democratic \uc2 AGs\uc2  prevail, we'll likely see more partisan challenges to \uc2 FDA\uc2  approvals, such as for Covid vaccines or Alzheimer's treatments. Regulating drugs isn't the business of the courts any more than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230409ej49002xl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
