\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Wisconsin Supreme Court Election Shows  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Continues to Sway Voters; Victory by  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 -rights supporter Judge Janet Protasiewicz underscores Republicans' challenges in swing states\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto and Shannon Najmabadi \par \uc2 1,171 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 April 2023\par 23:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 More than 1.8 million voters cast votes in Tuesday's election in Wisconsin, more than in any spring election not coinciding with a presidential primary going back to at least 2000, according to an unofficial tally provided by Riley Vetterkind. An earlier version of this article incorrectly said more people cast votes in the election than in any spring election going back to at least 2000. (Corrected on April 6)\par \par \uc2 Liberals on Tuesday gained their strongest hold on Wisconsin's highest court in decades, the latest in a string of electoral victories for Democrats in politically mixed states in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have played a central role.\par \par \uc2 Judge Janet Protasiewicz, a candidate with strong backing from the Democratic Party who openly touted her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/wisconsin-voters-to-decide-on-state-supreme-court-swing-seat-tuesday-d2445959?mod=hp_lead_pos6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won a crucial swing seat on the court }}}\uc2  by 11 percentage points in Tuesday's vote. It was the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/wisconsin-voters-to-decide-nations-most-expensive-state-court-election-97db287d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most expensive state supreme court race }}}\uc2  in U.S. history, in which money poured in from wealthy donors and national groups, including \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  and \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin Supreme Court candidates are technically nonpartisan, but Alan Ball, a \uc2 Marquette University\uc2  history professor who tracks the court, said it could now "display a liberal propensity unprecedented in the state's modern era." Academics who follow the court said it has been at least 15 years since liberals held considerable sway on the court and decades since they clearly had the upper hand. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
