\par \par \uc2 Judge Janet Protasiewicz touted her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the campaign. PHOTO: Kayla Wolf for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 During the campaign, Wisconsin Democratic leaders cast the race between Judge Protasiewicz and the conservative candidate, Daniel Kelly, as a referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, comparing it to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-abortion-vote-results-11659440554"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last summer's vote in Kansas }}}\uc2 . Voters in a referendum there soundly rejected a proposal to eliminate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections from the state constitution, the first concrete indication since Roe v. Wade was overturned last June that the issue could resonate with voters even outside liberal-leaning states.\par \par \uc2 Since then voters in other conservative-leaning or politically mixed states, including Michigan, Kentucky and Montana, have also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-rights-supporters-prevail-in-midterm-ballot-measures-11667986139"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dealt defeats to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  opponents }}}\uc2  in referendums on the issue. Political analysts credit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with delivering Democrats better-than-expected results in the midterms last November.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, Democrats say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped to energize liberal voters to come out and vote for Judge Protasiewicz in an off-year election when turnout is typically low. They also said it helped draw some independent and Republican voters to their side.\par \par \uc2 More than 1.8 million voters, or some 39% of Wisconsin's voting age population, cast votes in Tuesday's election, more than in any spring election not coinciding with a presidential primary going back to at least 2000, according to an unofficial tally provided by Riley Vetterkind, with the Wisconsin Elections Commission.\par \par \uc2 "That was a common theme that I was hearing at the doors from unlikely individuals. Like it wasn't just women or young people, it was allies who were seeing this impact their wives or their partners or their daughters," said Sarah Godlewski, the newly appointed state secretary of state who until recently led Women Win Wisconsin, a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  group that did canvassing in the race.\par \par \uc2 Conservatives say they were disappointed that their efforts to paint Judge Protasiewicz as a judicial activist, who would decide cases based on her personal views instead of the constitution, didn't resonate more with voters.\par \par \uc2 "It's a bit of a shock, the margin especially," said Nate Nelson, the Wisconsin executive director of American Majority Action, a conservative group that did voter outreach. "Obviously, there's a lot of work to be done for conservatives in the state."\par \par \uc2 The race wasn't just about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Another critical issue at stake was the state's electoral maps, which Judge Protasiewicz called "rigged."\par \par \uc2 Ads funded by Mr. Kelly's supporters sought to focus the race on crime, criticizing sentences Judge Protasiewicz had doled out to violent offenders as a lower-court judge and saying in one ad she "puts our families at risk."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kelly had his own weaknesses as a candidate. He was appointed to fill a vacancy on the Wisconsin Supreme Court in 2016 but lost an election in 2020 to retain his seat. Since then Mr. Kelly worked as a lawyer for the Republican Party, and Judge Protasiewicz sought to connect him to failed efforts to overturn Joe Biden's victory in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin's Supreme Court is expected to hear a challenge in the coming months to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an 1849 law in the state banning most abortions }}}\uc2 . The law has been dormant for decades, but since the overturning of Roe v. Wade last June its status has been unclear.\par \par \uc2 In the past, judicial candidates have often demurred about discussing their views on issues that could come before the courts. But Judge Protasiewicz's campaign ran ads saying she believes "women should have the freedom to make their own decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 She said, however, that despite her personal views she would have to hear the legal arguments in the case about whether the 1849 law should be upheld.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kelly was backed by prominent antiabortion organizations but he generally avoided discussing his views on the issue. He criticized his opponent for talking openly about her personal beliefs. "That's one of the things that's been a huge difference between my opponent and I about talking about our personal politics," he said in a radio interview before the vote. "Personal politics have nothing to do with the work of the court."\par \par \uc2 Political analysts said the Wisconsin race underscores that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't fading from the minds of voters and could continue to be an issue in the 2024 election as well.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How will the election of the new Supreme Court justice change Wisconsin? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 "This is an issue that I think Republicans have yet to figure out how to address," said Amy Walter, a political analyst at the Cook Political Report with Amy Walter.\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion groups say that it isn't \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that are hurting conservative candidates but rather their unwillingness to forcefully voice their opposition to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 "It's clear that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a top issue and it isn't going away. That's why it's imperative that pro-life candidates are clear about where they stand on what limits they support and contrast those limits with the extremism of the other side," said Kelsey Pritchard, state public affairs director for Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, which supported Mr. Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Joe Barrett and Ben Kesling contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Shannon Najmabadi at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:shannon.najmabadi@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shannon.najmabadi@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230405ej450050l\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
