\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Idaho Legislature Votes to Restrict  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Travel by Minors; Bill is one of first efforts by Republican lawmakers to address cross-border travel to obtain the procedure\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto and Jennifer Calfas \par \uc2 541 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 March 2023\par 18:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Idaho's legislature this week passed a bill making it a crime to assist minors in traveling to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a novel attempt by a Republican-led state to restrain residents from seeking the procedure in states where it is legal.\par \par \uc2 The bill creates a new offense of "\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trafficking," punishable by two to five years in prison. It would apply in circumstances in which a minor seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't have parental consent to do so.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of the bill say it protects parental rights.\par \par \uc2 Idaho Rep. Kevin Andrus, a Republican sponsor of the bill, said lawmakers recognized that some Idaho citizens will travel to other states to seek the procedure. "We have no issue with people making those decisions that fit them best," Mr. Andrus said. "It's when parents are uninformed of their children's activity that we get concerned."\par \par \uc2 Opponents say the bill violates a widely accepted right for U.S. citizens to travel to other states and participate in activities that are legal there. "We're opening a really dangerous Pandora's box with these travel bans," Rep. Ilana Rubel, a Democrat, said on the Idaho House floor Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Since the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-does-overturning-roe-v-wade-mean-explained-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade  }}}\uc2  last June, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now largely inaccessible in about one-third of the states, including Idaho, either because a ban is in place or clinics have stopped offering the procedure. That has spurred more women {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/women-encounter-abortion-delays-as-clinics-draw-patients-from-out-of-state-f40e318b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to travel  }}}\uc2  to states where the procedure remains legal.\par \par \uc2 Idaho borders a handful of states, including Oregon and Washington, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 where \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access is currently available  }}}\uc2 . Governors of those states and nearby California have said they aim to be safe havens for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. They have made efforts to shield providers from liability for providing the procedure to out-of-state residents, and to provide funding to assist with travel and other expenses.\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion leaders have said Democratic-led states are recruiting women to obtain abortions. Conservative lawmakers face challenges in acting on those concerns because of the legal right to interstate travel.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  , who was part of the Supreme Court majority that overruled Roe, said in a concurring opinion that he believed restricting travel would be unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Before the overturning of Roe, four states\u8212\'20\'14Arizona, Arkansas, Missouri and Oklahoma\u8212\'20\'14passed laws to prevent people from traveling with a minor for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a nearby state with less restrictive parental consent laws, according to \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2  , a state policy analyst at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  , which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Ms. Nash said it is unclear if any of those laws were ever enforced.\par \par \uc2 The Idaho bill now heads to the desk of Republican Gov. \uc2 Brad Little\uc2  . The governor has signed most antiabortion bills that have crossed his desk, but previously {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/idaho-governor-signs-six-week-abortion-ban-based-on-texas-model-but-voices-concerns-11648071555"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has expressed reservations about untested approaches.  }}}\uc2  A spokeswoman for the governor declined to comment Friday on whether he would sign the bill into law.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jennifer.calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jennifer.calfas@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jennifer Calfas at \par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230331ej3v005pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
