\par \par \uc2 The nation's capital is subject to congressional oversight because of a provision in the Constitution. PHOTO: Eric Lee for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The city that hosts the most powerful government in the world doesn't have much power.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's decision to side with Republicans and some Democrats in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-says-he-wont-veto-bill-blocking-d-c-crime-law-d26e6b54"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocking changes to the District of Columbia's crime code  }}}\uc2  is the latest in a long string of interventions regarding contentious local laws. Federal lawmakers say they are exercising needed oversight for the federal district, while local elected officials argue Congress is undercutting the democratic process to score political points.\par \par \uc2 After local D.C. voters legalized marijuana in 2015, Congress stopped efforts to allow the sale of it. A city needle-exchange initiative and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services for poor women were also defunded by Congress at various points. Congress has even intervened on regulations regarding building height in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/washington-d-c-office-market-feeling-ill-effects-of-remote-work-11674534492"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the low-slung city  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The nation's capital, home to about 700,000 residents, is subject to congressional oversight thanks to a provision in the Constitution that put the district under federal control. The city was granted limited home rule in 1973 with a mayor and district council, and advocates have unsuccessfully pushed for statehood to gain voting representation in Congress and full home rule. For decades, license plates have carried the motto "Taxation Without Representation" as a protest of the city's political status.\par \par \uc2 "The history is that the District has been used for political purposes," said D.C. Council Chairman \uc2 Phil Mendelson\uc2  , a Democrat who supported the crime code. He worries that the pace of meddling will only increase, noting that the council is considering naming a street after former Mayor Marion Barry that he fears will attract congressional scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 "It's now up to Congress to save our nation's capital from itself," said Rep. \uc2 Andrew Clyde\uc2  (R., Ga.) last month in making the case for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/washington-d-c-to-overhaul-criminal-code-despite-mayors-objections-council-member-says-11672879962"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overruling D.C. officials on the crime code  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under current law, the \uc2 D.C. Council\uc2  must submit all legislation to Congress for approval. Any objection comes in the form of a joint resolution disapproving a law, which is then fast-tracked to a vote. Blocking the D.C. code would mark the first time in decades that a city law has been formally overturned, as Congress instead typically passes riders on appropriation bills to nullify D.C. laws or block the city from taking specific steps on contentious issues such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or drug policy.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have typically harbored few qualms about using their authority to meddle in Washington's laws. While most Democrats back D.C. statehood and often side with the leaders of the heavily Democratic city, the developments on the crime code show that they will sometimes join Republicans in overruling Washington's local officials as well.\par \par \uc2 Local leaders see that as a betrayal that underscores the need for full legislative autonomy.\par \par \uc2 "This is an absolute travesty for statehood," said D.C. Council member Charles Allen, a proponent of the revised criminal code, speaking Friday on WAMU radio.\par \par \uc2 The issue of self-governance for Washington came to a head on Thursday when Mr. Biden went to Capitol Hill and told a meeting of Senate Democrats he would drop the White House's previously stated objections and instead back Republican efforts to block D.C.'s crime law.\par \par \uc2 It was a surprise turnabout. Mr. Biden, who supports statehood and has made the defense of democracy more broadly a central tenet of his administration, had initially signaled support for D.C.'s law on the grounds that the city ought to be able to govern itself.\par \par \uc2 "Denial of self-governance is an affront to the democratic values on which our nation was founded," the Biden administration wrote in a formal statement of administration policy dated Feb. 6. "Congress should respect the District of Columbia's autonomy to govern its own local affairs."\par \par \uc2 But after 31 House Democrats {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/republicans-move-to-block-changes-to-washington-d-c-criminal-code-11675953834"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted with Republicans  }}}\uc2  to block the D.C. law, and a handful of senators said they would side with the GOP as well, Mr. Biden relented. He said that he, too, had qualms about the code, and would sign the bill blocking the code if it came to his desk.\par \par \uc2 The overhaul to the city's criminal code has been in the works for more than a decade. Among other provisions, it updates references to steamboats, stables, outhouses and the care of livestock. But the local law also contains politically difficult measures for national Democrats, including reducing maximum and minimum penalties for crimes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/washington-d-c-police-fight-a-rise-in-carjackings-and-blame-covid-19-11614261600"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 such as carjacking  }}}\uc2  and unauthorized possession of firearms, just as the city is seeing a surge in murders and car thefts.\par \par \uc2 Washington Mayor Muriel Bowser, who opposed the criminal-code revision but also wanted Congress to stay out of the matter, said the council should have made the changes she sought on contentious aspects of the code. She had vetoed the code but had been overridden by the council in a 12-1 vote.\par \par \uc2 "This is not a new issue," she said about congressional oversight, on WAMU radio Friday. "We work with what we have."\par \par \uc2 Rather than formally overturning laws, Congress usually opts to block appropriations instead.\par \par \uc2 Republican President George H.W. Bush vetoed a series of appropriations bills for the District of Columbia over funding for abortions. "Although I do not object to the funding provided by the bill, its language concerning the use of funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unacceptable," the then-president {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.senate.gov/legislative/vetoes/messages/BushGHW/HR5517-Hdoc-102-339.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote, explaining his 1992 veto  }}}\uc2  of one such measure.\par \par \uc2 Republicans attached a measure to a spending bill that limited the city's ability to fund needle-exchange programs in the late 1990s.\par \par \uc2 In 2011, Democratic President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  agreed to a budget deal with Republicans that curtailed funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the District, prompting a street protest that led to the arrest of then-Mayor Vincent C. Gray along with several \uc2 D.C. Council\uc2  members.\par \par \uc2 Interference in Washington's rules isn't always about politically charged national issues. Congress passed a resolution to overrule a D.C. law in 1991, blocking the city's ambitions to build an office and residential complex near the \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2  headquarters that would have exceeded the city's 110-foot height limit for that project.\par \par \uc2 Aside from the resolution related to the building height, Congress has only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://crsreports.congress.gov/product/pdf/IN/IN12119"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned D.C. laws two other times  }}}\uc2 , according to the \uc2 Congressional Research Service\uc2  . The first law to be overturned was the Location of Chanceries Act of 1979, a council effort to block the expansion of embassies in certain parts of the city, because of residents' concerns about traffic and parking. The second to be overturned was the Sexual Assault Reform Act of 1981.\par \par \uc2 Write to Annie Linskey at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:annie.linskey@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 annie.linskey@wsj.com  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230304ej340018h\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
