\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone has emerged as a battleground after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. PHOTO: Natalie Behring for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 A dozen states are suing the Food and Drug Administration over what they say are unnecessary regulations on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit was filed Friday in the U.S. District Court for the Eastern District of Washington by the Democratic attorneys general of Washington, Oregon, Nevada, Delaware, Arizona, Illinois, Connecticut, Colorado, Vermont, New Mexico, Michigan and Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 It alleges that federal regulations on mifepristone are "excessively burdensome\u8230\'20\'26despite ample evidence that the drug is safer than Tylenol."\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone works by blocking a hormone necessary to sustain a pregnancy. It is taken alongside misoprostol, which causes uterine contractions, and is approved for use in the first 10 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-is-an-abortion-pill-leaked-supreme-court-decision-11651775689"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Medication abortions }}}\uc2  accounted for 54% of abortions in the U.S. in 2020, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and tracks national and state statistics. In Washington state, almost 60% of abortions are medication abortions, according to the state's attorney general.\par \par \uc2 "The federal government has known for years that mifepristone is safe and effective," Washington state Attorney General Bob Ferguson said in a statement. "In the wake of the Supreme Court's radical decision overturning Roe v. Wade, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is now exposing doctors, pharmacists and patients to unnecessary risk. The \uc2 FDA\uc2 's excessive restrictions on this important drug have no basis in medical science."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  didn't immediately reply to a request for comment on the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit comes amid growing legal challenges over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the U.S. following the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court's ruling }}}\uc2  in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, which returned the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 's legality to states.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 patchwork of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access }}}\uc2  has emerged across the U.S. since the June 2022 decision. About a dozen states have enforced bans on nearly all abortions, while others have moved to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access through state laws and executive orders. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/little-known-abortion-pill-maker-faces-scrutiny-11656939602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  pills }}}\uc2  have emerged {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/story/states-are-next-battlegrounds-for-abortion-pill-access-8abde763"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as a battleground }}}\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents last year sued the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , seeking to undo federal approval of mifepristone. In January, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-pill-restrictions-in-two-states-challenged-in-lawsuits-11674656653"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two lawsuits }}}\uc2  challenged states that restricted medication abortions. The suits argued that states can't impose restrictions on the medication that are stricter than those of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved mifepristone, in combination with misoprostol, for medication abortions in 2000, according to the lawsuit filed Friday, and the drug has been safely used more than 5 million times in the U.S. since its approval.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, the federal agency {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/fda-allows-women-to-get-abortion-pill-by-mail-11639698022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 relaxed restrictions }}}\uc2  around the drug, allowing patients to get mifepristone without a visit to a hospital or doctor. In January, it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-pills-to-be-available-more-widely-under-new-fda-rules-11672789765"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued changes }}}\uc2  that allowed bricks-and-mortar pharmacies to dispense the drug for the first time.\par \par \uc2 But the attorneys general argue that the restrictions on prescribing and dispensing mifepristone are unnecessary and harmful.\par \par \uc2 They say that of the more than 20,000 drugs approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , only 60 are subjected to restrictions known as Risk Evaluation & Mitigation Strategies, or REMS, which limit who can prescribe and dispense the medication.\par \par \uc2 They also allege that required documentation of the patient's use of the drug decreases telehealth appointments and puts patients in "danger of violence, harassment, and threats of liability amid the growing criminalization and outlawing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in other states."\par \par \uc2 "REMS restrictions are supposed to apply to inherently dangerous drugs, including opioids like fentanyl, and high-dose sedatives used by psychiatric patients, among others," the attorneys general said in a statement Friday.\par \par \uc2 Write to Talal Ansari at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:talal.ansari@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 talal.ansari@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230224ej2o005xx\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
