\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 State Battles on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access Are Ahead in the New Year; Uncertain political backdrop, ongoing litigation loom over states whose  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  laws remain in flux\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 1,164 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 December 2022\par 20:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 State legislatures across the country are preparing to convene for the first time since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ended federal constitutional protections for \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , with Republicans facing questions about whether to adopt new restrictions after the party's tepid performance in the midterm elections.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More than 20 states were expected to ban }}}\uc2  many or most abortions following the Supreme Court's decision this summer in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization. But in roughly half of those places, efforts to impose new restrictions have hit legal and political obstacles. Now, lawmakers are grappling with how to respond to an electoral season that saw voters signal resistance to sweeping prohibitions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including in some conservative-leaning states.\par \par \uc2 "We haven't really felt the full impact at the state level," said \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , a state policy analyst at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Nationally, Republicans didn't perform as well as expected in the midterms, and political analysts believe voter concern about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access was one reason why. Some GOP candidates lost to Democrats who made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a central issue in their campaigns. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents also were on the losing end of five state ballot initiatives in the midterms, as well as a proposed ballot measure in August to eliminate protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Kansas constitution.\par \par \uc2 Yet Republicans also strengthened their position in some states, including in North Carolina, Ohio and Florida, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is being closely watched, buoyed in some circumstances by a socially conservative base that supports stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 "We have a lot of reason to be optimistic about our ability to advance protections for life at the state level after this election," said Stephen Billy, vice president of state affairs at Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, one of the country's largest antiabortion groups.\par \par \uc2 Voters didn't punish conservative politicians for new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions but for their timidity in defending them, Mr. Billy said.\par \par \uc2 Florida has emerged as a major focus for antiabortion groups, who are pushing for a bill banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy. The state is an outlier in the Republican-dominated South in permitting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Thus far, however, Florida Republican leaders have been hesitant to commit publicly to the details of any new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation. That includes Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who said after the Dobbs decision in June that he would "work to expand pro-life protections." But the governor, who is widely viewed as a potential 2024 presidential candidate, has given few specifics about what type of limits he would support.\par \par \uc2 Legislative leaders there say they are also waiting for the Florida Supreme Court to rule on whether the 15-week ban violates the state's constitution.\par \par \uc2 The case is one of several in which state judges are poised to play a major role in 2023. State supreme courts in South Carolina, North Dakota, Idaho and Kentucky also are expected to issue key rulings that could help determine whether their state constitutions protect a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina, Republicans fell just short in the midterms of a legislative supermajority that could override the Democratic governor's expected veto of any new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits. Nonetheless, House Speaker \uc2 Tim Moore\uc2  has said he is confident Republicans could join with a handful of Democrats to pass some new veto-proof \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The state, where the procedure is legal until about 20 weeks of pregnancy, has become a regional access point as most of its southern neighbors have banned many or most abortions.\par \par \uc2 "We staved off that supermajority by one vote but that really underscores how thinly \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is hanging on in North Carolina," said Jenny Black, president and chief executive of Planned Parenthood South Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 Nebraska, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently legal up to 20 weeks of pregnancy, is expected to be another key battleground. Gov. \uc2 Pete Ricketts\uc2 , a Republican, said in August he wouldn't call a special session to pass a ban on most abortions after 12 weeks of pregnancy because there wasn't enough support in the Legislature. In a letter to Mr. Ricketts, 30 state senators said they would support the legislation\u8212\'20\'14three lawmakers short of the 33 needed to pass the law, according to the governor.\par \par \uc2 It is possible Republicans could get closer to that number next year.\par \par \uc2 In some states where Republicans dominate local politics, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-opponents-consider-how-far-to-press-after-end-of-roe-v-wade-11656273349"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  opponents face challenging fights }}}\uc2  as divisions emerge within the party about how far to push. In South Carolina, where Republicans have majorities in both chambers of the legislature and the governorship, legislators have tried and failed since the summer to pass a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to internal divisions.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in a number of states are also debating whether to soften some existing state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in response to public pushback after these laws went into effect. Most \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans passed in recent years didn't include exceptions for rape and incest because proponents said they were trying to protect as many unborn lives as possible. Now prominent Republicans in states including Florida, Tennessee and Ohio are urging their party to add more robust exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, Republicans have indicated that they don't expect to pass a proposed near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the lame-duck session this year. Instead they have said they would focus on clarifying {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/doctors-struggle-with-navigating-abortion-bans-in-medical-emergencies-11665684225"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 exceptions for medical emergencies }}}\uc2  in the state's existing six-week ban, which is currently blocked by the courts. Legislators could still take up a total ban next year, although Republican Gov. \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  has urged legislators not to pass anything that won't win the support of a majority of Ohio voters.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How do you expect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will shift in the coming year? Join the conversation below.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates say they are planning to gather signatures to put forward a ballot initiative to amend Ohio's constitution to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the next couple of years.\par \par \uc2 State Republicans, meanwhile, are proposing a constitutional amendment that, if passed by the legislature and approved by voters, would require a supermajority of 60% of the vote rather than a simple majority to pass any new constitutional amendments.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -policy experts cautioned against assuming that antiabortion Republicans will significantly soften their stance at the state level, where the midterm elections didn't deal the same disappointments as they did to GOP candidates in federal races.\par \par \uc2 "Their voters rewarded them for what they have already been doing about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mary Ziegler, a law professor at \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Calfas contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/state-battles-on-abortion-access-are-ahead-in-the-new-year-11670444095"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 State Battles on \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Access Are Ahead in the New Year }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221207eic7005pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
